<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Ah Canul</h1><p class="st-meta">1441 CE – 1541 CE (approx.) · capital Calkiní</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Maya province of north-western Campeche governed by the Canul lineage, whose history survives in the Codex of Calkiní.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Canul lineage traced its origins to warriors from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-coast-of-mexico">Gulf Coast</a> who served the rulers of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/league-of-mayapan">Mayapan</a>. After the fall of the League of Mayapan around 1441, they settled in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campeche">Campeche</a> and established a province with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calkini">Calkiní</a> as its leading town.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ah Canul was governed by a council of lords of the Canul lineage rather than a single supreme ruler, each heading a group of towns. Its people farmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and traded with the coast. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codex-of-calkini">Codex of Calkiní</a>, written in Maya in the colonial period, records its lineages and the coming of the Spanish.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1541 the Canul lords submitted peacefully to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-montejo-the-younger">Francisco de Montejo the Younger</a>, and the province was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-yucatan">Province of Yucatán</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calkini">Calkiní</a> is now a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campeche">Campeche</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ah-canul.jpg" alt="Ah Canul" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Cacicazgos_mayas.jpg: Jaontiveros Mexico_States_blank_map.svg: Sémhur derivative work: ecelan (talk) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cacicazgos_mayas_-_es.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1441 CE – 1541 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Calkiní</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Maya religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Yucatec Maya</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Canul province</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1491&amp;focus=ah-canul" aria-label="Ah Canul on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2254 1424 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ah Canul lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2574 1680L2591 1670M2574 1680L4910 989M2574 1680L2590 1658"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2591 1670h0M4910 989h0M2590 1658h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2574 1680h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2574 1680h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Calkiní. <a href="/atlas?year=1491&amp;focus=ah-canul">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
